# QA Re-alignment Document

## Issues

- [x] `src/tools/inspect/parseSymbols/index.ts` line 32–49 — `truncateBeforeBody` misidentifies the `>` in `=>` as a closing generic angle bracket, decrementing depth to -1 before the body `{` is reached. Block-body arrow functions (e.g. `const fn = (x: number): number => { return x; }`) return a signature that contains the full implementation body.
  Traces to: undesired outcome 1 — "Returning the function body along with the signature."
  Misalignment: implementation-drift

- [x] `src/tools/inspect/parseSymbols/index.ts` lines 164–175 and 150–162 — `truncateBeforeBody` is applied to `InterfaceDeclaration` and `ClassDeclaration` nodes, stripping everything after the opening `{` of the body. For interfaces this leaves only `export interface Config` — no members. An agent cannot determine the contract from a name alone.
  Traces to: desired outcome 3 — "An agent that calls inspect and gets a result back has enough information to decide whether to read the full file or to use the symbol based on its contract alone."
  Misalignment: implementation-drift

- [x] `src/tools/inspect/index.test.ts` — no test case covers a block-body arrow function (e.g. `const fn = (x: number): number => { return x; }`). The existing arrow test uses only an expression-body arrow (`a + b`), which never exercises the body-truncation path and does not trip the depth-tracking bug.
  Traces to: undesired outcome 1 — "Returning the function body along with the signature."
  Misalignment: test-gap

- [x] `src/tools/inspect/index.test.ts` — no test case asserts on interface or class signature content. The interface and class tests check only `kind` and `name`, so the lossy truncation of interface members passes tests silently.
  Traces to: desired outcome 3 — "enough information to decide whether to read the full file or to use the symbol based on its contract alone."
  Misalignment: test-gap

## Retro

Both bugs share a root cause: `truncateBeforeBody` was designed for function and method declarations (where `{` at depth 0 unambiguously opens the body), but was applied uniformly to all symbol kinds without considering the structural differences.

**Block-body arrow:** The `>` in `=>` is a language keyword token, not a generic closing bracket. The depth tracker has no way to distinguish `=>` from `>`. Either the depth model needs to be extended (scan for the literal `=>` sequence before tracking depth, then advance past it), or `truncateBeforeBody` should not be called on the arrow initializer at all — instead, the signature should be built from the text up to and including the `=>` token only.

**Interface and class body:** These declarations' bodies ARE their contract. The fix is to not call `truncateBeforeBody` on `InterfaceDeclaration` nodes — include the full text. For `ClassDeclaration` the tradeoff is different: a class body can be very large, so truncating before `{` (and noting "see file for members") may be intentional. However, the current behavior strips everything silently with no indication that members exist. This needs a deliberate decision: either include the full class body, or append a truncation marker like ` { ... }` so the agent knows members exist but were not shown.

**What would have caught this earlier:** A plan-stage checklist item requiring the implementor to test every symbol kind with a signature-content assertion (not just `kind` and `name`) would have caught the interface case. A block-body arrow test case in the plan's test checklist would have caught the arrow case. The plan lists "signature truncation" as a test concern (item 7, test case 10) but scopes it only to function declarations — it does not require the same check for arrow functions with block bodies.
